  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't checked the rule's 5xx query against the actual data (details below).\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The data view uses `timestamp` as its time field. Each document is one web request with:\n- **Request:** `url`, `request`, `extension`, `referer`\n- **Response:** `response` (the HTTP status code, stored as a string with a `.keyword` subfield) and `bytes`\n- **Client:** `clientip`, `agent`, `machine.os`, `machine.ram`\n- **Location:** `geo.src`, `geo.dest`, `geo.coordinates`\n- **Other:** `host`, `tags`, `memory`, `hour_of_day`\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-18c18e78 Web logs overview\"** (id `eff-18c18e78-web-logs-overview-b7451a657edd`, at http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-18c18e78-web-logs-overview-b7451a657edd). It has seven panels:\n- Total requests (metric)\n- Unique visitors, counted as distinct `clientip` (metric)\n- Total bytes served (metric)\n- Requests over time, split by response code (bar)\n- Response code breakdown (pie)\n- Top 10 requested URLs, with request count and average bytes (table)\n- Top 10 source countries (bar)\n\n**3. Rule \"EFF-18c18e78 5xx spike\"** (id `d951baa8-0aec-4ca6-a100-3af7ed07f11d`). It's an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs` that runs every minute. It counts requests whose `response.keyword` starts with \"5\" over the last 5 minutes of `timestamp`, and fires when the count is more than 10.\n\n**Not verified:** I tried to check the 5xx query against the index directly, but Kibana's console proxy rejected the API key (401). The query matches the field's format, since codes are stored as text like `503`. To confirm it, look at the rule's execution history in Stack Management \u2192 Rules after it has run a few times.\n\nThe rule has no notification action attached. It will record alerts but won't send them anywhere until you add a connector, such as Slack or email.",
